e>5</Volume><Issue>4</Issue><PubDate><Year>2017</Year><Month>Dec</Month><Day>11</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Journal of developmental biology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>J Dev Biol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Engineering the Drosophila Genome for Developmental Biology.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">16</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3390/jdb5040016</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>The recent development of transposon and CRISPR-Cas9-based tools for manipulating the fly genome in vivo promises tremendous progress in our ability to study developmental processes. Tools for introducing tags into genes at their endogenous genomic loci facilitate imaging or biochemistry approaches at the cellular or subcellular levels. Similarly, the ability to make specific alterations to the genome sequence allows much more precise genetic control to address questions of gene function.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Korona</LastName><ForeName>Dagmara</ForeName><Initials>D</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Genetics, University of Cambridge, Downing Street, Cambridge CB2 3EH, UK. dk500@cam.ac.uk.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Koestler</LastName><ForeName>Stefan A</ForeName><Initials>SA</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-7223-8291</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Genetics, University of Cambridge, Downing Street, Cambridge CB2 3EH, UK. sak76@cam.ac.uk.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Russell</LastName><ForeName>Steven</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Genetics, University of Cambridge, Downing Street, Cambridge CB2 3EH, UK. s.russell@gen.cam.ac.uk.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</Publication