eId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">23449360</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">26208947</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2015</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>24</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2020</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>30</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">1873-9601</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>9</Volume><Issue>3</Issue><PubDate><Year>2015</Year><Month>Sep</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Journal of cell communication and signaling</Title><ISOAbbreviation>J Cell Commun Signal</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Come together, right now&#x2026;.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>283</StartPage><EndPage>284</EndPage><MedlinePgn>283-4</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s12079-015-0301-y</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Editing the genome using approaches like TALEN and siRNA are already well tested. The new kid on the block is CRISPR-Cas9. CRISPR-Cas9 is rapidly evolving with impressive refinements for specificity, eliminating off-target effects, and versatility. One can adjust constructs and conditions to produce opposite effects on the genome and for a specific purpose. The nuances of the system, the means to significantly reduce off-targeting, and numerous applications are now emerging rapidly. This B&amp;B commentary looks forward into how the CRISPR-Cas9 tool might serve the CCN field.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yeger</LastName><ForeName>Herman</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Program in Developmental &amp; Stem Cell Biology, The Hospital for Sick Children, Toronto, ON, Canada. hermie@sickkids.ca.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D0164