</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">20180094</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">34939826</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2024</Year><Month>07</Month><Day>24</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2024</Year><Month>07</Month><Day>24</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1557-7430</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>41</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2022</Year><Month>Jan</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>DNA and cell biology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>DNA Cell Biol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>How CRISPR/Cas9 Gene Editing Is Revolutionizing T Cell Research.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>53</StartPage><EndPage>57</EndPage><MedlinePgn>53-57</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1089/dna.2021.0579</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats (CRISPR)/Cas9 allows for precise gene targeting in mammalian cells, including T cells, allowing scientists to disrupt or edit specific genes of interest. This has enabled immunologists to investigate T cell functions as well as opened the path for novel therapeutics involving gene editing of T cells <i>ex vivo</i> before transferring these back to patients to increase T cell efficacy. This review outlines how CRISPR/Cas9 has transformed T cell research allowing immunologists to rapidly probe the roles of genes in T cells thus paving the way for novel therapeutics. Furthermore, this review describes how these tools reduce the requirement for genetic mouse models, while increasing the translational potential of T cell research.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Johansen</LastName><ForeName>Kristoffer Haurum</ForeName><Initials>KH</Initials><Identifi